            <div xml:id="d531">

                <pb facs="0623" n="613" xml:id="pg613"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.24/1662: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-10-13T18:00:00+07:00">October 13, 1943—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 13—12:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1936. Consul [at] Tihwa reported that Provincial Chairman informed him October 9
                    that Soviets base objection to transshipment of war material to China on desire
                    to avoid arousing Japanese suspicion, that Soviet Ambassador [at] Chungking said
                    he would prefer to have to refer to Moscow Chinese proposal that some military
                    goods be transported, and that the “plan can not be effected at present”.</p>
                <p>Foregoing refers to Department’s 1407, October 1.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
